        "t2": {
          "issueOrPullRequest": {
            "__typename": "PullRequest",
            "id": "PR_kwDOErHI988AAAABHvKNzg",
            "number": 1582,
            "title": "fix(query): decode valid escapes next to a malformed one",
            "body": "when a query value has one malformed escape, `decodeURIComponent` throws and `parseQuery` keeps the whole value undecoded, so the valid escapes stay encoded too. a stray `%` is enough:\n\n```ts\napp.get(\"/search\", (event) => ({\n  getQuery: getQuery(event).q,\n  searchParams: event.url.searchParams.get(\"q\"),\n}));\n// GET /search?q=caf%C3%A9+50%\n// main: {\"getQuery\":\"caf%C3%A9 50%\",\"searchParams\":\"café 50%\"}\n// this: {\"getQuery\":\"café 50%\",\"searchParams\":\"café 50%\"}\n```\n\n`URLSearchParams` and `node:querystring` both decode the valid escapes and keep the malformed ones as-is. now the catch falls back to that instead of skipping, for keys and values. the happy path doesn't change.\n\nthe test compares `parseQuery` with `URLSearchParams` on a few malformed inputs, it fails on main.\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n  * Query strings with malformed percent-encoded keys or values are now decoded more consistently, including when valid and malformed escapes appear together.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
